# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
# The sleep wallpapers (docs in sleep/README.md) are the user's own pictures:
# they are put in a folder on the store, which preinit mounts read-write at
# /kindle-us while the policy is not loaded yet (preinit/PreInit.c,
# testfile_handover_store), so the folder and its files carry the vfat label.
# The display HAL reads them when the screen goes off
# (gralloc/framebuffer.cpp, wallpaperSleepLocked); nothing else SurfaceFlinger
# does touches the store.
#
# Why not an allow on vfat: the base policy forbids it. In AOSP 8.1
# system/sepolicy/private/surfaceflinger.te:
#     neverallow surfaceflinger sdcard_type:file rw_file_perms;
# vfat is in sdcard_type (public/file.te: `type vfat, sdcard_type, fs_type,
# mlstrustedobject;`), so any read allow from surfaceflinger to vfat:file is
# refused by checkpolicy -- the first version of this file was exactly that and
# did not compile (env-auditor, 2026-11-10). A type of our own is the way out.
#
# Why the kernel needs a patch for genfscon: on this kernel (Lab126 3.0.35)
# security/selinux/hooks.c sets SE_SBGENFS in selinux_set_mnt_opts() only for
# proc, debugfs, sysfs and pstore; without that flag inode_doinit() gives a vfat
# inode the superblock's label instead of looking the path up (hooks.c, the
# `default:` branch with `sbsec->flags & SE_SBGENFS`), so a genfscon rule for
# vfat is ignored and everything stays `vfat`. The fix is two lines in that
# function, adding vfat to the same test (env-auditor / T13 kernel patch 1061).
# With it, genfscon below labels /kindle-us/sleepwall and its files.
#
# Why this rule is ordered correctly: genfs paths are matched first-prefix by
# the kernel (security_genfs_sid), so `/` and `/sleepwall` both match a file in
# this folder. The compiled policy keeps the rules sorted by path length,
# longest first (checkpolicy/policy_define.c, define_genfs_context_helper), so
# `/sleepwall` is tried before `/`. That is the single-policy path this device
# builds (`PRODUCT_FULL_TREBLE_OVERRIDE := false`); a Treble-style CIL build
# sorts genfscon by path ascending instead and would put `/` first.
#
# genfscon paths are relative to the root of the mounted filesystem: dm-0 is
# mounted at /kindle-us, so `/sleepwall` is /kindle-us/sleepwall. This also
# means the folder must not be reached through a bind mount -- a bind mount's
# root is its own `/` and the label would fall back to vfat.
#
# The search on vfat:dir is for the walk into the mount, not for reading files:
# looking up "sleepwall" checks search on the parent, which is the vfat root
# inode (the mount root), before the kernel steps into the labelled directory.
# The neverallow above is on files, so this one compiles; it does not let
# SurfaceFlinger open anything on the store except the wallpapers, whose type
# grants only read.
type pw3_sleepwall_file, file_type, data_file_type;
allow surfaceflinger vfat:dir search;
allow surfaceflinger pw3_sleepwall_file:dir r_dir_perms;
allow surfaceflinger pw3_sleepwall_file:file r_file_perms;
# Android reaches the store through the FUSE daemon that vold starts
# (vold auto-trans: vold, sdcardd_exec, sdcardd). That daemon opens the files on
# the store itself, so with no allow on this type its stat/readdir fails and
# Android shows /sdcard/kindle/sleepwall as an unknown 0-byte file instead of a
# directory (T21). These are the same macros AOSP public/sdcardd.te grants for
# sdcard_type, so the folder behaves like the rest of the user area: list, put
# and delete wallpapers from Android. The display HAL keeps only read access and
# nothing here lets it write.
allow sdcardd pw3_sleepwall_file:dir create_dir_perms;
allow sdcardd pw3_sleepwall_file:file create_file_perms;
